#!/bin/bash
# A stand-in for the real `codex`, so the e2e suite can run a session on a
# provider whose harness pocket-dev cannot read.
#
# IT REPAINTS UNBIDDEN, and that is the whole point. A tab on a provider with no
# transcript reads as status 'unknown' forever, so before the unreadAxis enum
# every frame it painted WHILE THINKING advanced the server's unread counter
# with nothing to ever close the guard. The row then read "Waiting on you" and
# lit the attention badge, which is the strongest signal the UI has, fired by a
# session doing the opposite of needing the user. Nothing in the suite could
# produce that state before a provider was selectable: `cat` only speaks when
# spoken to.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT AN ATTEMPT TO BE REAL CODEX, and do not "improve" it into
# one. Measured 2026-09-07 against codex-cli 0.151.0 driven from a bare pty: it
# emits 613 bytes of terminal capability queries and then BLOCKS FOREVER waiting
# for replies a dumb pty never sends:
#
#   \e[>4;0m  \e[>7u  \e]10;?\e\\  \e]11;?\e\\
#
# (kitty-keyboard support, then foreground and background colour). xterm.js
# answers those, which is why real codex works in pocket-dev at all, but a
# fixture harness does not, so wiring the real binary in here would hang at
# startup and read as a broken test. This is a plain frame emitter for the same
# reason stub-bin/claude is a plain script.
#
# It ignores its argv, including --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox,
# which the real command line carries.
#
# NOTE what this stub does NOT claim: whether real Codex actually emits bytes
# while thinking is UNVERIFIED. 'none' is right either way. If it repaints, this
# is the false summons avoided; if it is silent, counting its bytes could only
# ever have added noise, and the row still says "not tracked".

printf 'CODEX-STUB-READY\r\n'

# Repaint on a timer, the way a TUI redraws while it works. Cursor-addressed and
# in place, so this is a repaint rather than accumulating output.
n=0
while true; do
  n=$((n + 1))
  printf '\033[HFrame %d\033[K\r\n' "$n"
  sleep 0.2
done
